  "summary": "Health-tech investment and AI implementation dominated today's coverage, with multiple stories highlighting the push to scale artificial intelligence across clinical settings—from agentic EMR systems for physical therapy to Medicare's prior authorization experiments, though concerns persist about whether AI is being deployed responsibly enough, particularly in mental health care. Major funding activity continued with StimCardio raising $30 million for atrial fibrillation therapy and several notable October funding announcements, while health system consolidation remained active with Integris Health joining Risant Health to accelerate value-based care. Policy development is accelerating with proposed Senate bills addressing health AI regulation and reimbursement, though questions mount about implementation details like remote patient monitoring oversight and the adequacy of current frameworks. On the clinical front, the FDA approved a generic HIV prevention shot to expand access in lower-income countries and granted expedited approval to Inovio's DNA-based immunotherapy for a rare tumor, while a Washington state death marked the first U.S. bird flu fatality in a year. Healthcare system pressures continued to surface, with uncompensated care expected to rise following Medicaid disenrollments and nurses at Brigham and Women's announcing an open-ended strike, underscoring ongoing workforce and financial tensions in the sector.",
